<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Fukuoka Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Fukuoka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Kuroda domain of Chikuzen in northern Kyushu, which shared with Saga the duty of guarding Nagasaki.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagamasa">Kuroda Nagamasa</a>, son of Hideyoshi's strategist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-yoshitaka">Kuroda Kanbei</a>, fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikuzen-province">Chikuzen</a> province in 1600 with about 520,000 koku. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> castle beside the old merchant city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakata">Hakata</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Kuroda ruled for the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. The twin cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> and merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakata">Hakata</a> formed one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu's</a> largest urban centres. From 1641 the domain alternated with Saga in guarding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan's</a> only port for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Chinese trade. The Confucian scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaibara-ekken">Kaibara Ekken</a> served the domain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagahiro">Kuroda Nagahiro</a> promoted Western learning in the 19th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> joined the imperial side in 1868. In 1870 its officials were found counterfeiting money, and the last lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagatomo">Kuroda Nagatomo</a>, was dismissed in 1871 shortly before the domains were abolished; it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka-prefecture">Fukuoka Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/fukuoka-domain.jpg" alt="Fukuoka Domain" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fukuoka_Castle_Simonohasi_Otemon_gate.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fukuoka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kuroda Nagamasa, Kuroda Nagahiro, Kuroda Nagatomo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chikuzen Domain, Kuroda Domain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=fukuoka-domain" aria-label="Fukuoka Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8010 959 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Fukuoka Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8330 1215L8530 1145M8330 1215L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8330 1215h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8330 1215h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Fukuoka. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=fukuoka-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
